WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.750
Course registration opens.

2
00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.510
One seat is left in a popular

3
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.010
elective, and two students click

4
00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.390
register in the same millisecond.

5
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.040
Both get a confirmation.

6
00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.650
How did the system sell

7
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.210
the same seat twice?

8
00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.610
Today: what a transaction guarantees,

9
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:22.150
how isolation levels allow or prevent

10
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.950
anomalies, and what changes when data

11
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.390
lives on many machines.

12
00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.030
A transaction groups operations

13
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.050
so they behave as one unit.

14
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.020
ACID names its guarantees: atomicity,

15
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.820
consistency, isolation and durability.

16
00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:41.630
Either everything

17
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.670
commits, or nothing does.

18
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.560
Both requests read one seat left.

19
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.800
Both subtract one and write zero.

20
00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.910
Without proper isolation, each

21
00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.790
overwrites the other, and the

22
00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.570
count never shows minus one.

23
00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.770
One fix is an atomic conditional update

24
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.330
that only succeeds while seats remain.

25
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.320
Another is serializable isolation, which

26
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.060
aborts one of the two transactions.

27
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.660
Either way, exactly one

28
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.880
student is enrolled.

29
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.350
Databases offer weaker isolation

30
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.850
levels for speed, such as read

31
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.990
committed and snapshot isolation.

32
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.010
Each permits some anomalies.

33
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.200
Only serializable behaves as if

34
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.320
transactions ran one at a time.

35
00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.670
When data is replicated and the

36
00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.450
network splits, a system must choose.

37
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.400
It can stay available and risk

38
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.120
inconsistent answers, or stay

39
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.360
consistent and refuse some requests.

40
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.100
That is the CAP trade-off, and students

41
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.720
should argue it from real cases.

42
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:51.520
Quick check!

43
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.200
The double booking happened because

44
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.920
both transactions read the same seat

45
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.600
count before either wrote.

46
00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.130
Which ACID property failed?

47
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.120
Isolation.

48
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.700
Each transaction was atomic

49
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.240
and durable on its own.

50
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.600
The problem was how they interleaved.

51
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.200
In the app, open the pipeline lab.

52
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.810
Make the load fail halfway and retry: rows

53
00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.890
written twice show why a load must be one

54
00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.310
atomic transaction, or safe to repeat.

55
00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:27.070
Your turn.

56
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.220
During a network partition, a bank's

57
00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.220
ledger must never show two different

58
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:32.640
balances.

59
00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:34.470
What must it give up?

60
00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.200
Availability: some requests are

61
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.320
refused until the partition heals.

62
00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.870
Remember: transactions are units of work,

63
00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.010
isolation levels trade anomalies for

64
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.010
speed, and partitions force a choice.

65
00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.370
Try it in your next lecture: give

66
00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.470
two volunteers cards with the

67
00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.050
steps of two transactions.

68
00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.780
The class calls out an interleaving

69
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.760
and records the final seat count.

70
00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.670
Then add a lock and try again.
